Hell Creek was once a warm, swampy coast with rivers running to a shallow sea. When animals died near the rivers, [[@floods-why|floods]] buried them fast in mud. That mud slowly turned to rock, and the bones turned to [[fossil|fossils]].

Today the land is dry badlands, with bare hills and gullies. Wind and rain wear the soft rock away, so bones poke out of the hillsides. That makes it a good place to look, though finds are still rare.

{{story:fossils-form}}

Out in the open, plastic has a harder life. The Sun's [[ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] light weakens the long chains of [[molecule|molecules]] that make it strong. A bucket left on a roof for years turns pale and brittle and finally cracks when you touch it.

Each crack makes more pieces. Scientists call anything smaller than 5 millimetres, about the size of a grain of rice, a microplastic. They have found microplastics in farm soil, river mud, city dust and even on mountains. Some farms add them by accident, when thin plastic sheets used to cover crops tear and get ploughed into the [[@soil-made|soil]].

{{story:ocean-plastic}}

[[@glaciers|ग्लेशियर]] बर्फ़ की नदियाँ हैं, जो ढलान पर रेंगती हैं, अक्सर एक दिन में एक मीटर से भी कम। बर्फ़ में जमे पत्थर ज़मीन को ऐसे खुरचते हैं जैसे कोई विशाल रेगमाल। ग्लेशियर घाटी को एक किनारे से दूसरे किनारे तक भर देते हैं, इसलिए वे तल के साथ उसकी दीवारें भी तराशते हैं।

पिछले हिमयुग में ग्लेशियरों ने ऐसी कई पहाड़ी घाटियाँ भर रखी थीं, जिनमें आज बर्फ़ नहीं है। अमेरिका के कैलिफ़ोर्निया की [[@yosemite|योसेमिटी]] घाटी इसकी मशहूर मिसाल है, जहाँ [[granite|ग्रेनाइट]] की लगभग एक किलोमीटर ऊँची चट्टानें हैं और नीचे समतल तल। छोटे बग़ली ग्लेशियरों ने उथली घाटियाँ काटीं, जो अब मुख्य घाटी की दीवार पर बहुत ऊपर ही ख़त्म हो जाती हैं। इन "लटकती घाटियों" से नाले झरने बनकर गिरते हैं। योसेमिटी झरना लगभग 740 मीटर नीचे गिरता है।

{{story:yosemite}}

Stars form in the coldest, densest corners of nebulae. A clump of gas starts to shrink under its own gravity, gets hotter in the middle and eventually starts fusing hydrogen. Our own Sun formed this way about 4.6 billion years ago, from a cloud that has long since drifted away.

The most famous picture of a star nursery is the Pillars of Creation, three tall columns of gas and dust in the Eagle Nebula. The [[@hubble|Hubble]] Space Telescope photographed them in 1995. In 2022 the [[@webb-telescope|Webb telescope]] looked again in [[infrared|infrared]] light, which passes through dust, and revealed baby stars glowing inside the pillars. The tallest pillar is about four [[lightyear|light-years]] high.

{{story:colour-mix}}

विशाल स्क्विड की आँखें किसी भी जानवर की सबसे बड़ी आँखों में से हैं, लगभग 27 सेंटीमीटर तक चौड़ी। यह [[@eyes-see|तुम्हारी आँखों]] से लगभग दस गुना ज़्यादा चौड़ी हैं। गहराई में रोशनी बहुत कम होती है, इसलिए बड़ी आँखें जितनी भी रोशनी है, उसे बटोरने में मदद करती हैं।

वैज्ञानिक मानते हैं कि उसकी आँखों का एक ख़ास काम भी हो सकता है। उसकी सबसे बड़ी दुश्मन और [[predator|शिकारी]], स्पर्म व्हेल, विशाल होती है, और तैरते हुए वह ऐसे नन्हे जीवों को हिला देती है जो रोशनी की चमक छोड़ते हैं। विशाल स्क्विड की बड़ी आँखें शायद दूर से ही वह चमक पकड़ लेती हैं, जिससे उसे भागने का मौका मिल जाता है।

{{story:ocean-zones}}

Near [[@world-greenland|Greenland]] and [[@antarctica|Antarctica]], seawater gets very cold. When sea ice forms, it leaves salt behind in the water. Cold, salty water is heavy, so it sinks to the bottom and creeps along the sea floor. More water flows in at the surface to replace it.

This sets up a slow loop through all the oceans that scientists sometimes call the global conveyor belt. Water that sinks in the North Atlantic may spend around a thousand years in the deep before it rises again somewhere far away. Saltiness plays a big part, as you can read in [[@why-sea-salty|why the sea is salty]].

{{story:wind-blows}}

In Gehlaur village in Bihar, a rocky hill stood between Dashrath Manjhi's home and the nearest town. To reach doctors, villagers had to walk the long way around. After his wife was badly hurt crossing it, he took a hammer and chisel to the hill.

He worked for about 22 years, from around 1960 to 1982. In the end he cut a path about 110 metres long through solid rock, wide enough for carts. A walk of dozens of kilometres shrank to just a few. People now call him [[@india-mountain-man|the Mountain Man]], and a Hindi film was made about his life in 2015.

{{story:banyan}}

Gerardus Mercator was a Flemish mapmaker. His 1569 map keeps compass directions as straight lines. A sailor could draw a line from port to port, read off the angle and steer by it. That made it valuable for [[navigation|navigation]] on the [[@trade-routes|spice routes]] and beyond.

The price was size. To keep directions true, Mercator stretched the map more and more towards the poles. [[@antarctica|Antarctica]] looks like a giant white continent stretched along the bottom, and Alaska seems bigger than Mexico, though Mexico is larger.

{{story:maps-history}}

Researchers have watched thousands of children play a "peeking game". A child sits facing a wall while a toy is placed behind them. The adult leaves, saying "don't peek". A hidden camera shows that most children peek. When asked, many of them then say they didn't.

Psychologist Kang Lee at the University of Toronto found that children start trying out lies at about 2 or 3 years old. By 4, most do it sometimes. This isn't a sign of a bad child. To lie, you have to understand that other people have minds of their own and can't see inside yours. It's one of the skills a [[@brain-development|growing brain]] is learning. Lee also found that children who were asked to promise to tell the truth before answering were more likely to be honest. Hearing a story that praised honesty helped too, while stories that warned of punishment did not.

{{story:brain-development}}

Hypatia taught geometry, astronomy and [[philosophy|philosophy]], especially the ideas of Plato. She is said to have lectured in public, wearing a scholar's cloak, and city leaders came to her for advice.

Later writers say she wrote explanations of three hard mathematical books. One was the *Arithmetica* of Diophantus, a collection of puzzles about unknown numbers that is an early ancestor of [[@algebra|algebra]]. Another was about conic sections, the curves you get by slicing a cone at different angles. The third helped readers with Ptolemy's tables of the movements of the stars and planets. Copies of these works may survive inside later books, but no one can be sure which parts are hers.

{{story:great-libraries}}

2011 में वैज्ञानिक मार्क चैंगिज़ी ने ध्यान दिलाया कि उँगलियों की झुर्रियाँ ढलान पर बहती नदियों की धाराओं जैसी दिखती हैं। उन्होंने कहा कि शायद ये पकड़ते समय उँगलियों के सिरों से पानी बाहर निकाल देती हैं, जैसे कार के टायर के खाँचे।

2013 में इंग्लैंड की न्यूकैसल यूनिवर्सिटी की एक टीम ने इस विचार को परखा। स्वयंसेवकों ने गीले कंचे एक डिब्बे से दूसरे में रखे, कभी चिकनी उँगलियों से, कभी झुर्रीदार उँगलियों से। झुर्रीदार उँगलियों ने काम जल्दी किया। सूखे कंचों में कोई फ़र्क नहीं पड़ा। पर बाद के कुछ अध्ययनों में यह फ़ायदा नहीं मिला। इसलिए पकड़ वाला विचार मशहूर तो है, पर साबित नहीं।

{{story:tyres}}

1953 में एक अमेरिकी आदमी, जिसे विज्ञान की दुनिया एच.एम. के नाम से जानती है, गंभीर दौरे रोकने के लिए ऑपरेशन से गुज़रा। [[surgeon|सर्जनों]] ने उसके दिमाग के कुछ हिस्से निकाल दिए, जिनमें दोनों तरफ़ के हिप्पोकैम्पस का बड़ा हिस्सा भी था। दौरे तो कम हो गए, पर एच.एम. अब नई लंबी यादें नहीं बना पाता था। वह किसी से गपशप कर लेता, और कुछ मिनट बाद भूल जाता कि उससे मिला भी था।

मनोवैज्ञानिक ब्रेंडा मिलनर ने सालों तक उसकी जाँच की। उन्होंने पाया कि वह अब भी नए हुनर सीख सकता था, जैसे आईने में देखकर चित्र बनाना, जबकि उसे कभी याद नहीं रहता था कि उसने अभ्यास किया है। उसके मामले से पता चला कि हुनर और तथ्य दिमाग में अलग-अलग तरीकों से सहेजे जाते हैं।

{{story:storytelling-india}}

In 1969, in Jaipur, the [[surgeon|surgeon]] P. K. Sethi teamed up with a craftsman named Ram Chandra Sharma to make a better artificial foot. Western limbs were expensive and built for people wearing shoes and sitting on chairs. Many Indians walk barefoot, squat and sit cross-legged.

The Jaipur Foot was made of rubber and wood, cheap and flexible, and fitted in a single day. A charity in Jaipur has given it free to well over a million people in many countries, including landmine survivors. It changed what [[@prosthetics|artificial limbs]] could look like.

{{story:prosthetics}}

The card's trick is called [[@induction-stoves|induction]]. When a [[@magnets-how|magnetic]] field changes near a coil of wire, it pushes electricity through the wire. Electric [[@toothbrush|toothbrushes]] and some [[@chargers|phone chargers]] use the same idea.

{{story:magnets-how}}

The reader's field is weak and fades very quickly with distance. That's deliberate. At about 4 cm, the card gets enough power to wake up. A few steps away, it gets nothing at all. This short range is what makes NFC different from [[@bluetooth|Bluetooth]] or Wi-Fi, which are meant to reach across a room.

Jagadish Chandra Bose was a physics professor at Presidency College in Calcutta. He built his own equipment to make very short radio waves, now called microwaves, and invented sensitive detectors to catch them.

In a public demonstration in Calcutta in 1895, he is said to have sent waves through walls and across a hall. At the far end they rang a bell and set off a small charge of gunpowder. Bose was more interested in science than in business and showed little interest in [[@patents|patents]]. In 2012, the engineering society IEEE honoured his early work as a milestone in the history of electrical engineering.

{{story:radio-waves}}

In 1939 two young Stanford University graduates, Bill Hewlett and David Packard, rented a house at 367 Addison Avenue in Palo Alto. In the small garage behind it, they built their first product, an audio oscillator, a machine that makes test sounds. One of their early customers was the Walt Disney studio, which used them while making the film Fantasia.

Hewlett-Packard grew into one of the world's biggest technology companies. In 1989 the state of California named the garage "the Birthplace of [[@silicon-valley|Silicon Valley]]" and made it an official historic landmark.

{{story:homes-world}}

Philo Farnsworth grew up on a farm in Idaho, in the United States. He later said that at 14, while ploughing a field in straight rows, he had a sudden thought. A picture could be scanned the same way, line by line, using a beam of electrons instead of a spinning disc. He sketched his idea for his science teacher.

On 7 September 1927, in a small laboratory in [[@san-francisco|San Francisco]], Farnsworth sent his first electronic image. It was a single straight line. Soon he could show moving images clearly. A long patent battle followed with the giant company RCA, where Vladimir Zworykin was building his own electronic camera. In 1939, RCA agreed to pay Farnsworth for using his [[@patents|patents]].

{{story:eyes-see}}

For about 600 years, kings kept a royal menagerie at the Tower. In 1252, King Henry III was given a white bear, probably a polar bear, from Norway. It was led on a chain to fish in the Thames. An elephant, a gift from France, arrived a few years later. The animals were moved out in the 1830s, and many went to the new London Zoo, an early chapter in the story of [[@zoos-history|zoos]].

Today, the Tower is famous for ravens. Legend says that if they ever leave, the kingdom will fall. At least six live there, looked after by a Ravenmaster. Ravens are clever birds, as smart as [[@crow-smart|crows]], and some have escaped or been sent away for bad behaviour.

{{story:crow-smart}}

For many people, New York was their first sight of America. The [[@statue-liberty|Statue of Liberty]], a gift from France, was dedicated in 1886 in the harbour. From 1892 to 1954, about 12 million [[@ellis-island|immigrants]] passed through the inspection station on nearby Ellis Island. Many stayed in the city, crowding into apartments and building new lives.

{{story:statue-liberty}}

Immigrants still shape the city today. Hundreds of languages are spoken in New York. Queens is one of the most varied places on Earth, and Jackson Heights has streets of Indian, Bangladeshi, Nepali and Tibetan shops. You can find [[@biryani|biryani]], [[@food-momos|momos]] and dosas a few blocks apart.

Yellowstone has more than 10,000 hot springs, geysers, mud pots and steam vents. Over 500 of them are geysers, roughly half the world's total. Rain and snowmelt seep underground, get heated by the hot rock, and burst back up. You can see how pressure builds in [[@geysers-hot-springs|geysers and hot springs]].

[[@old-faithful|Old Faithful]] erupts roughly every 60 to 110 minutes, shooting water up to about 55 m high. Steamboat Geyser, also in the park, is the world's tallest active geyser and can blast water over 90 m into the air, higher than a 25-storey building.

{{story:yellowstone-wolves}}

चावल का एक दाना स्टार्च से भरा होता है, जो नन्हे-नन्हे सख़्त कणों में जमा रहता है। गर्म पानी में ये कण पानी सोखकर फूल जाते हैं, कभी-कभी अपने आकार से कई गुना। कुछ फट भी जाते हैं और उनका स्टार्च पानी में घुल जाता है। इसीलिए पास्ता का पानी धुँधला हो जाता है और धीमी आँच पर पकती खीर गाढ़ी होती जाती है।

यही तवे पर सिकती रोटी के आटे में होता है और [[@pressure-cooker|प्रेशर कुकर]] में पकते आलू में भी। नरम, फूला हुआ स्टार्च शरीर के लिए तोड़ना भी कहीं आसान होता है। तुम्हारे [[@stomach-digest|पेट और आँतों]] को कच्चे अनाज के मुक़ाबले पके स्टार्च से ज़्यादा [[energy|ऊर्जा]] मिलती है।

{{story:fire-discovery}}

[[bacteria|Bacteria]] grow fastest at warm temperatures, between about 5 °C and 60 °C. In an Indian summer, the kitchen counter sits right in this zone. Cooked food left out for hours can become unsafe even if it looks and smells fine.

Health agencies advise putting leftovers in the fridge within about two hours, and sooner on hot days. A fridge should be colder than 5 °C. Reheated food should be steaming hot all the way through, rather than just warm on top. Rice, dal and meat dishes need extra care.

{{story:soap-germs}}
